    body: "<table><thead><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Objects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>-2</td><td>Service account <code>music-platform-sa</code>, ExternalSecret <code>music-platform-env</code></td></tr><tr><td>-1</td><td>Postgres Service and StatefulSet</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>App Deployment and Service</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Ingress and HPA</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A wave moves on when Argo believes the resources are healthy, not merely when the API accepted them. A StatefulSet is healthy when its pod is Ready. Postgres will not become Ready until <code>music-platform-env</code> contains <code>POSTGRES_USER</code>, <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code>, and <code>POSTGRES_DB</code>, because those are <code>secretKeyRef</code>s. So wave 0 does not start the app Deployment until the database pod is Ready, as long as Argo is waiting on health.</p><p>ExternalSecret health depends on Argo’s health check for that CRD. Treat the init container as the lock you can see in <code>kubectl describe pod</code>: it prints “Waiting for PostgreSQL” until <code>nc -z postgres 5432</code> succeeds.</p>"
